window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][67] = "<h1>Appendix C. Competency matrix</h1>\n<p>The ten competencies that interview loops probe, the three bands used by every chapter's rubric (Baseline, Strong, Standout), the weight each carries in interviews, and where the book teaches it. It is the scoring rubric behind the self-assessment in C01 and the mock-interview scorecards in C34. Company differences are in the company lens of each chapter and in C33.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-0-how-to-read-this-document\"></span>\n<h2>C.0 How to read this document</h2>\n<p><strong>Bands</strong> (used by every chapter rubric and by Appendix E):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>What an interviewer would write</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Passes a screen on this dimension. Unlikely to clear a hiring decision by itself.</td>\n<td>&quot;Knew the vocabulary; answer was generic or unstructured.&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Clear hire signal on this dimension.</td>\n<td>&quot;Structured, made explicit choices, justified them.&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Top of the pool. The dimension is a reason to hire.</td>\n<td>&quot;Changed how I think about the problem; would want on my team.&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Provenance of the bands.</strong> Companies do not publish scoring rubrics at this resolution. The behaviors below are an <strong>Original</strong> synthesis, built from public hiring attributes (C.1) and from the structure of the questions commonly reported by candidates and prep sources. Where a claim is about what a specific company does, it carries <code>Public</code> or <code>Inferred</code>. Time-sensitive claims carry <code>[as of 2026-10, verify]</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-1-company-signal-map-calibration-only-not-a-rubric\"></span>\n<h2>C.1 Company signal map (calibration only, not a rubric)</h2>\n<p>I checked public material on the Google APM process. Results were third-party prep sites and a university career-center page, <strong>not Google itself</strong>, so process details are <code>Inferred</code> and may be stale.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>Public signals</th>\n<th>Reported format</th>\n<th>Label</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Google (APM)</strong></td>\n<td>Google publicly describes hiring on four attributes: general cognitive ability, role-related knowledge, leadership, and &quot;Googleyness.&quot;</td>\n<td>Two-year rotational program per prep sources. Reported loop: phone screen, then several rounds across product sense, execution/analytical, behavioral and estimation, then committee review and often a final conversation. Reported acceptance rate is very low in some years, source unclear.</td>\n<td>Attributes: <code>Public</code> <code>[verify]</code>. Format: <code>Inferred</code> <code>[as of 2026-10, verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Meta (RPM)</strong></td>\n<td>Rotational PM program referenced by prep sources.</td>\n<td>Commonly described as product sense, execution (analytical) and leadership rounds.</td>\n<td><code>Inferred</code> <code>[as of 2026-10, verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Amazon</strong></td>\n<td>Amazon publishes its Leadership Principles and ties interviews to them.</td>\n<td>Behavioral questions mapped to principles, plus product and technical depth. Entry routes include technical PM roles <code>[verify]</code>.</td>\n<td>Principles: <code>Public</code>. Routes and format: <code>Inferred</code> <code>[verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Microsoft</strong></td>\n<td>Publicly emphasizes a growth-mindset culture.</td>\n<td>Early-career PM entry points and loop format vary by org.</td>\n<td>Culture: <code>Public</code>. Format: <code>Inferred</code> <code>[verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Apple, Netflix</strong></td>\n<td>No formal rotational APM program known to me.</td>\n<td>Entry usually via other roles or experienced hiring.</td>\n<td><code>Inferred</code> <code>[verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stripe, Atlassian, Uber, others</strong></td>\n<td>Stripe and Atlassian reported to run APM-style programs.</td>\n<td>Varies.</td>\n<td><code>Inferred</code> <code>[verify]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Implication for design:</strong> the competency set below is the intersection that every one of these loops probes under different names. Company differences go in per-chapter <strong>Company lens</strong> sections, not in separate chapters.</p>\n<hr>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-the-ten-competencies\"></span>\n<h2>C.2 The ten competencies</h2>\n<p>Interview weight is <code>Inferred</code> from the reported round structure (C.1): <strong>High</strong> = a dedicated round at most companies; <strong>Med</strong> = embedded in rounds; <strong>Low</strong> = occasional.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-1-product-sense-product-sense-tier-1-weight-high\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.1 Product sense (<code>product-sense</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>High</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Includes:</em> designing for a user, improving an existing product, designing for a new market, critiquing a product.\n<em>Scored in:</em> dedicated product sense/design round (<code>Inferred</code>); on the job: choosing what to build for whom.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Restates the prompt. Picks a user group by demographics (&quot;college students&quot;). Lists features. Offers one solution. Names a metric without linking it to the goal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Clarifies product, goal and constraints in under two minutes. Segments users by need or behavior and picks one with a stated criterion. Ranks pain points by frequency and severity. Offers three solutions that differ in kind, not in degree. Compares them on impact, effort and risk, recommends one, and defines a success metric with a guardrail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Reframes the problem around the underlying job, not the surface request. Ties the choice to the company's actual advantage. Surfaces a non-obvious user or insight and says why it is non-obvious. Kills one of their own ideas on stated grounds. Names second-order effects (abuse, trust, ecosystem, cannibalization). Adjusts live when the interviewer pushes back, without abandoning the structure. Closes with a phased plan and what evidence would change the decision.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> feature list with no user; solution fixed before the problem is defined; &quot;make it better&quot; metrics.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-2-user-research-and-discovery-discovery-tier-1-weight-me\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.2 User research and discovery (<code>discovery</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>Med</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Scored in:</em> product sense round (user understanding), behavioral round (&quot;how did you learn what users needed?&quot;), occasionally a &quot;plan the research&quot; prompt (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;Run surveys and interviews.&quot; Treats what users say as what they need.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Separates problem, solution and feature. Picks method to fit the question (qual to find, quant to size). Writes non-leading questions. Names sampling and bias risks. Turns findings into a problem statement a team could act on.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Designs the cheapest test that could falsify the riskiest assumption. Contrasts stated versus observed behavior and explains the gap. States what the research cannot tell you. Shows a real decision that changed because of evidence, from their own work.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> asking users to design the product; research as ritual with no decision attached.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-3-problem-solving-problem-solving-tier-1-weight-high\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.3 Problem solving (<code>problem-solving</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>High</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Sub-skills:</em> structuring (MECE, issue trees, hypotheses), root-cause analysis, estimation.\n<em>Scored in:</em> analytical rounds, estimation rounds (reported as program-specific at Google, <code>Inferred</code>), and implicitly in every round.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Recites a named framework. Categories overlap or omit something obvious. Estimate has numbers but no stated assumptions; no sanity check.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Clarifies the question before structuring. Builds a tree cut along the dimension that matters for the decision, with non-overlapping, exhaustive branches. Prioritizes branches by likelihood and cost to check. States each estimation assumption aloud, uses round numbers, cross-checks with a second route.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Works hypothesis-first and says which branch they would test first and why. Chooses the cut because of the decision it supports. Quantifies which branch matters most. Reports estimates as ranges and says what would move them. Ends estimation with &quot;so what&quot;: what decision this number changes. Recovers visibly when an assumption is challenged.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> framework theater (naming a framework instead of using it); overlapping branches; precision without a sanity check.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-4-analytics-and-experimentation-analytics-tier-1-weight-\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.4 Analytics and experimentation (<code>analytics</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>High</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Includes:</em> choosing metrics, funnels and cohorts, diagnosing a metric change, experiment design, causal reasoning.\n<em>Scored in:</em> analytical/execution round (<code>Inferred</code>); on the job: reading dashboards and experiment readouts.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Names DAU/MAU and &quot;look at the funnel.&quot; Knows A/B tests exist. For a metric drop, jumps to a favorite cause.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Defines a north star with input metrics and guardrails; states each metric precisely (numerator, denominator, window, population). Diagnoses in a fixed order: check the data, then segment (time, platform, geography, cohort, funnel step), then separate internal from external causes, then form causal hypotheses. Designs an experiment: unit, randomization, duration, primary and guardrail metrics, decision rule.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Reasons causally: confounders, selection effects, sample-ratio mismatch, novelty effects, interference between users, Simpson's paradox. Chooses a fallback when a randomized test is impossible and states its weakness. Anticipates how a metric will be gamed. Balances short- and long-term effects. States the threshold at which they would ship, hold or roll back, before seeing results.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> metric lists without definitions; &quot;run an A/B test&quot; as the answer to everything; confusing correlation with the cause of a drop.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-5-execution-execution-tier-1-weight-high\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.5 Execution (<code>execution</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>High</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Includes:</em> prioritization, tradeoffs, PRDs, launch decisions, working with engineering and design.\n<em>Scored in:</em> execution round and behavioral round (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Lists steps. Cites RICE or MoSCoW by name; ranks by gut.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>States the objective and what &quot;done&quot; means. Scopes an MVP and says what was cut and why. Prioritizes with explicit criteria. Sequences work by dependency. Names risks with mitigations. Defines rollout stages, success criteria and a rollback trigger.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Decides under constraint with options: &quot;cut scope, move date, or add capacity; I recommend X because Y.&quot; Separates reversible from irreversible decisions. Instruments before launch. Sets kill criteria in advance. Handles &quot;engineering says 3x longer&quot; and &quot;launch metric regressed&quot; with a concrete decision path and the stakeholder communication to go with it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> plan without tradeoffs; every item &quot;P0&quot;; no decision rule for launch.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-6-technical-fluency-technical-tier-1-weight-med-high-for\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.6 Technical fluency (<code>technical</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>Med</strong> (High for technical-leaning roles)</h3>\n<p><em>Includes:</em> how products work, APIs, data, PM-level system design.\n<em>Scored in:</em> technical/system round where present, and implicitly via credibility with engineers (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Uses terms (API, database, cloud) loosely. Cannot trace a user action through the system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Walks a request end to end (client, network, service, data store, response). Explains APIs and data models at box-and-arrow level. Names latency, scale and reliability tradeoffs and what each costs the user. Covers privacy and security basics. Asks engineer-grade clarifying questions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Ties each technical choice to a user outcome and a cost. Finds the bottleneck and the likely failure mode. Gives order-of-magnitude capacity estimates. Frames build versus buy and tech-debt decisions as product decisions. Explains the same design to an engineer and to an executive at two levels.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> buzzword fluency; designing a system with no product requirements; hiding behind &quot;that's an engineering question.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-7-ai-product-thinking-ai-tier-1-weight-med-and-rising-in\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.7 AI product thinking (<code>ai</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>Med and rising</strong> <code>Inferred</code></h3>\n<p><em>Includes:</em> LLM products, RAG, agents, evals, safety, cost and latency.\n<em>Scored in:</em> AI-specific product questions where offered, and in any product design question where AI is the obvious tool (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;Add an LLM.&quot; Knows hallucination exists. No view on cost or failure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Decides whether AI is the right tool, and says why or why not. Defines the task, the data and the cost of a wrong answer. Proposes evals (offline set, online metrics). Names the quality, cost and latency tradeoff. Designs fallbacks and human review. Distinguishes prompting, retrieval and fine-tuning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Writes the eval before the feature. Designs a trust progression for autonomy. Works out unit economics at scale. Handles non-determinism in the UX. Names privacy, safety and compliance risks specific to the use case. States what is likely to change with better models and what will not.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> AI as decoration; demo-quality thinking with no eval; ignoring per-query cost.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-8-design-and-ux-design-ux-tier-2-weight-med\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.8 Design and UX (<code>design-ux</code>) — Tier 2 — weight: <strong>Med</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Scored in:</em> product sense round (flows, critique) and execution (working with design) (<code>Inferred</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Recites heuristics. &quot;Make it simpler.&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Critiques an interface against the user's goal, in priority order. Sketches a flow, including empty, error and loading states. Weighs speed against craft. Covers accessibility basics.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Ties each design issue to a metric or a user outcome. Designs for edge users (low bandwidth, low literacy, assistive tech). Spots dark patterns and declines them with a reason. Knows when the problem is not a design problem.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> critique by taste (&quot;I don't like it&quot;); redesigning without a user.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-9-strategy-and-gtm-at-apm-depth-strategy-gtm-tier-1-stra\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.9 Strategy and GTM at APM depth (<code>strategy-gtm</code>) — Tier 1 (strategy) / Tier 2 (GTM, pricing, growth) — weight: <strong>Med</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Scored in:</em> strategy/business round where present, otherwise within product sense (<code>Inferred</code>). No P&amp;L ownership expected at this level.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Lists competitors; fills a SWOT.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Answers &quot;should X enter Y?&quot; with criteria: market attractiveness, right to win, economics, risk. States a thesis and a recommendation with conditions. Explains positioning, a pricing model and distribution choices.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Identifies the source of advantage (distribution, data, switching costs, network effects). Anticipates competitor response. Sequences from a wedge to expansion. Sanity-checks unit economics. States what would prove the thesis wrong. For launches, names channel, message and the first 1,000 users.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> generic market description; strategy without a choice; ignoring incumbents' reaction.</p>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-2-10-behavioral-and-communication-behavioral-tier-1-weight\"></span>\n<h3>C.2.10 Behavioral and communication (<code>behavioral</code>) — Tier 1 — weight: <strong>High as a gate</strong></h3>\n<p><em>Scored in:</em> behavioral/leadership round, and in how every other answer is delivered (<code>Inferred</code>). Amazon makes the link to published principles explicit (<code>Public</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Stories lack specifics. &quot;We&quot; throughout; the candidate's role is unclear. No outcome, no learning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Each story states context, the decision they personally made, the action, the measurable result and what they would change. Answers the question asked. Under two minutes unless probed. Has stories for conflict, failure, influence without authority, ambiguity and customer focus.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Holds up under three levels of follow-up questions. Failure story shows a changed behavior, not just a lesson. Disagreement story shows respect for the other side and a real cost to the candidate. Maps stories to the company's stated attributes without sounding scripted. Adjusts length and depth to interviewer cues.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Failure signature:</em> rehearsed monologue; hero narrative with no cost; vagueness about personal contribution.</p>\n<hr>\n<span id=\"app-c-c-3-cross-cutting-layer-interview-craft-interview-craft-tier\"></span>\n<h2>C.3 Cross-cutting layer: interview craft (<code>interview-craft</code>) — Tier 1</h2>\n<p>Not a scored competency, but it multiplies all ten. It is a Tier 1 topic in this book (loop structure, company-specific formats, mock system).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Band</th>\n<th>Observable behaviors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Baseline</strong></td>\n<td>Starts answering immediately. No signposting. Runs out of time before recommending.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong></td>\n<td>Clarifies, then states the plan (&quot;I'll cover users, problems, solutions, metrics&quot;). Signposts. Spends the first third on definition and the last third on decision and metrics. Closes with a one-sentence recommendation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standout</strong></td>\n<td>Reads interviewer cues and reallocates time. Invites steering. Treats pushback as data. Keeps a visible thread from problem to metric. Stays calm when stuck and says how they would unstick themselves.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr>\n<span id=\"appendix-d\"></span>\n";
